WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.768
October, twenty twenty.

00:00:02.108 --> 00:00:04.311
An empty desert highway in Nevada.

00:00:04.651 --> 00:00:09.786
A car from a small American company is
about to claim a number no production car

00:00:09.786 --> 00:00:11.583
had ever officially reached.

00:00:11.923 --> 00:00:15.199
An average of more than five hundred
kilometres per hour.

00:00:15.539 --> 00:00:19.796
The video went online, and within days the
world had a new fastest car.

00:00:20.136 --> 00:00:23.846
Then people started timing that car
against landmarks on the road.

00:00:24.186 --> 00:00:28.740
What they found turned the record into one
of the biggest arguments in car history.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:34.126
And this November, a Chinese electric car
will try to break that same barrier, for

00:00:34.126 --> 00:00:35.049
real this time.

00:00:35.389 --> 00:00:40.161
To understand why five hundred is so hard,
we have to go back to a park outside

00:00:40.161 --> 00:00:42.698
Paris, in December, eighteen ninety-eight.

00:00:43.998 --> 00:00:48.338
A French car magazine organises a timed
run through the park at Achères.

00:00:48.678 --> 00:00:53.670
The winner is a count, Gaston de
Chasseloup-Laubat, in an electric car, at

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:55.828
sixty-three kilometres per hour.

00:00:56.168 --> 00:00:58.318
The first recognised land speed record.

00:00:58.658 --> 00:01:02.124
A Belgian racer, Camille Jenatzy, takes
that personally.

00:01:02.464 --> 00:01:07.080
Over the next four months, the two men
take the record from each other again and

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:07.426
again.

00:01:07.766 --> 00:01:09.836
Then Jenatzy builds something new.

00:01:10.176 --> 00:01:14.232
A torpedo-shaped electric car he calls La
Jamais Contente.

00:01:14.572 --> 00:01:16.115
The never satisfied.

00:01:16.455 --> 00:01:20.550
On the twenty-ninth of April, eighteen
ninety-nine, it becomes the first car ever

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:23.078
to go faster than one hundred kilometres
per hour.

00:01:23.418 --> 00:01:25.795
The first car to break a hundred was
electric.

00:01:26.135 --> 00:01:28.949
Remember that, because it matters at the
end of this story.

00:01:30.249 --> 00:01:34.436
For the next century, the fastest machines
on land stopped being cars you could buy.

00:01:34.776 --> 00:01:39.634
In nineteen ninety-seven, a British car
called Thrust SSC, pushed by two jet

00:01:39.634 --> 00:01:44.876
engines, broke the sound barrier in the
Nevada desert, at more than twelve hundred

00:01:44.876 --> 00:01:46.154
kilometres per hour.

00:01:46.494 --> 00:01:51.737
Its driver, a Royal Air Force fighter
pilot, is still the only person to have

00:01:51.737 --> 00:01:53.371
gone supersonic on land.

00:01:53.711 --> 00:01:55.691
But nobody can buy a jet car.

00:01:56.031 --> 00:01:59.943
So a second race began: the fastest car
you can actually buy.

00:02:00.283 --> 00:02:03.927
And that race has two rules that decide
everything in this story.

00:02:04.267 --> 00:02:08.703
Rule one: two runs, in opposite
directions, and the record is the average.

00:02:09.043 --> 00:02:10.979
That cancels out wind and slope.

00:02:11.319 --> 00:02:15.726
Rule two: the record car has to be the
same as the cars sold to customers.

00:02:16.066 --> 00:02:17.906
Keep those two rules in mind.

00:02:18.246 --> 00:02:21.127
Almost every fight that follows breaks one
of them.

00:02:22.427 --> 00:02:27.250
In nineteen ninety-eight, a driver named
Andy Wallace takes a McLaren F1 to a

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:29.881
Volkswagen test track in northern Germany.

00:02:30.221 --> 00:02:32.942
Three hundred and eighty-six kilometres
per hour.

00:02:33.282 --> 00:02:34.963
The record stands for years.

00:02:35.303 --> 00:02:39.777
Then Bugatti arrives with the Veyron, and
in twenty ten its Super Sport version

00:02:39.777 --> 00:02:41.873
averages four hundred and thirty-one.

00:02:42.213 --> 00:02:45.396
In twenty thirteen, Guinness takes that
record away.

00:02:45.736 --> 00:02:47.391
The reason was the speed limiter.

00:02:47.731 --> 00:02:52.329
On the record car it had been switched
off, but on the cars sold to customers it

00:02:52.329 --> 00:02:53.249
was switched on.

00:02:53.589 --> 00:02:54.914
That broke rule two.

00:02:55.254 --> 00:02:56.571
Bugatti pushes back.

00:02:56.911 --> 00:03:01.587
One week later, after an outside expert
review, Guinness gives the record back.

00:03:01.927 --> 00:03:05.318
A limiter, it decides, does not change
what the car is.

00:03:05.658 --> 00:03:06.876
The lesson was clear.

00:03:07.216 --> 00:03:08.509
The rules could bend.

00:03:09.809 --> 00:03:11.784
November, twenty seventeen.

00:03:12.124 --> 00:03:14.416
Koenigsegg does something no one had
tried.

00:03:14.756 --> 00:03:16.439
It closes a public highway.

00:03:16.779 --> 00:03:21.111
A stretch of Nevada State Route one sixty,
outside the town of Pahrump.

00:03:21.451 --> 00:03:24.716
A customer's Agera RS runs in both
directions.

00:03:25.056 --> 00:03:28.656
Four hundred and forty-seven kilometres
per hour, averaged.

00:03:28.996 --> 00:03:30.411
A new world record.

00:03:30.751 --> 00:03:32.987
Two years later, Bugatti goes faster.

00:03:33.327 --> 00:03:37.451
A Chiron Super Sport prototype hits four
hundred and ninety, at that same German

00:03:37.451 --> 00:03:38.018
test track.

00:03:38.358 --> 00:03:42.371
The driver is Andy Wallace, again,
twenty-one years after the McLaren.

00:03:42.711 --> 00:03:47.096
But Bugatti only drives in one direction,
so it breaks rule one.

00:03:47.436 --> 00:03:52.695
It is the fastest speed a road car had
ever reached, and it is not an official

00:03:52.695 --> 00:03:53.167
record.

00:03:53.507 --> 00:03:56.973
Five hundred was now just ten kilometres
per hour away.

00:03:57.313 --> 00:03:58.769
Someone was going to claim it.

00:04:00.069 --> 00:04:03.330
That someone was SSC, a small American
carmaker.

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:07.961
In October twenty twenty, its Tuatara runs
on that same Nevada highway.

00:04:08.301 --> 00:04:12.035
The claim: five hundred and thirty-three
kilometres per hour one way, and an

00:04:12.035 --> 00:04:13.706
average of five hundred and eight.

00:04:14.046 --> 00:04:16.380
A new world record, by more than sixty.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:20.480
Then the video goes online, and people
start to check it.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:23.530
They time the car between landmarks on the
road.

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:27.131
It seems to be going far slower than the
numbers on the screen.

00:04:27.471 --> 00:04:31.884
The company that supplied the GPS
equipment says it never validated the run.

00:04:32.224 --> 00:04:34.799
Guinness confirms none of its officials
were there.

00:04:35.139 --> 00:04:41.163
Within weeks, SSC admits the video was, in
its own words, substantially incorrect.

00:04:41.503 --> 00:04:45.146
It blames an editing mix-up, and promises
to run again.

00:04:45.486 --> 00:04:50.762
In January twenty twenty-one, on a runway
at the Kennedy Space Center, a Tuatara

00:04:50.762 --> 00:04:55.972
runs again, with independent GPS data and
one of its loudest critics invited to

00:04:55.972 --> 00:04:56.368
watch.

00:04:56.708 --> 00:04:59.636
Four hundred and fifty-five kilometres per
hour, averaged.

00:04:59.976 --> 00:05:02.299
Fast, but more than fifty below the claim.

00:05:02.639 --> 00:05:04.921
The five hundred wall was still standing.

00:05:06.221 --> 00:05:08.021
September, twenty twenty-five.

00:05:08.361 --> 00:05:11.296
A test track in northern Germany, at
Papenburg.

00:05:11.636 --> 00:05:17.797
A hypercar from Yangwang, the luxury brand
of China's BYD, called the U9 Xtreme.

00:05:18.137 --> 00:05:22.659
With Marc Basseng at the wheel, it reaches
four hundred and ninety-six point two

00:05:22.659 --> 00:05:23.790
kilometres per hour.

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:25.461
Faster than the Bugatti.

00:05:25.801 --> 00:05:31.333
One hundred and twenty-six years after La
Jamais Contente, the highest speed ever

00:05:31.333 --> 00:05:35.568
recorded by a production car belongs to an
electric car again.

00:05:35.908 --> 00:05:37.814
But it ran in one direction only.

00:05:38.154 --> 00:05:39.377
Rule one, again.

00:05:39.717 --> 00:05:42.170
Officially, nobody has beaten five
hundred.

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:47.496
BYD says the car is coming back this
November, to the same track, to go past

00:05:47.496 --> 00:05:48.349
five hundred.

00:05:48.689 --> 00:05:53.011
Reports from the first run say the driver
still had about one and a half seconds of

00:05:53.011 --> 00:05:54.000
full throttle left.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:55.314
So will it count?

00:05:55.654 --> 00:05:57.535
That depends on the two rules.

00:05:58.835 --> 00:06:02.006
So when the next number comes in, ask two
questions.

00:06:02.346 --> 00:06:03.586
Did it run both ways?

00:06:03.926 --> 00:06:05.964
And is it the car you can actually buy?

00:06:06.304 --> 00:06:08.953
Jenatzy called his car the never
satisfied.

00:06:09.293 --> 00:06:12.432
A hundred and twenty-seven years later,
nobody is.

00:06:12.772 --> 00:06:15.417
Follow the channel, and we'll tell you if
the wall falls.
